UID:68d3b6cf75c14b6f3a42@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20261124T003607Z
DTSTART:20261013T223000Z
DTEND:20261014T000000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Camila Paleo\, University of California\, Santa 
 Barbara - Temporary Jobs\, Lasting Credit? Evidence from Uruguay's Publi
 c Works Program
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Camila Paleo\, University of California\, Santa Barb
 ara\nTitle: "Temporary Jobs\, Lasting Credit? Evidence from Uruguay's Pu
 blic Works Program"\n\nPublic works programs are common in developing co
 untries\, but evidence on their long-run effects remains limited. We est
 imate the effects of Uruguay's Oportunidad Laboral\, a temporary public 
 employment program\, on participants' financial well-being. Leveraging l
 ottery-based assignment and linked administrative records from the Centr
 al Bank of Uruguay\, we show that participation sharply increases formal
  employment and earnings during the program\, with no persistent formal-
 sector labor market effects after exit. In contrast\, formal credit use 
 rises persistently: by 8 percentage points during participation and by 8
 .4 percentage points in the 15-month post-program period\, consistent wi
 th temporary formal employment relaxing barriers to financial inclusion.
  However\, credit expansion also increases repayment risk. Delinquency r
 ises by 2.4 percentage points during the program and by 10.7 percentage 
 points afterward\, with effects persisting 31 months after exit. Effects
  are larger for transgender applicants\, smaller in high-unemployment mu
 nicipalities\, and delinquency effects are concentrated among new borrow
 ers. The results show that public works programs can affect welfare thro
 ugh financial markets\, but credit expansion without sustained earnings 
 may increase financial vulnerability. JEL Classification Codes: G51\, I3
 8\, J68\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-camil
 a-paleo-university-california-santa-barbara
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-camila-paleo-
 university-california-santa-barbara
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:b3bbecaa8de4c9c6ce31@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20261124T003607Z
DTSTART:20261015T223000Z
DTEND:20261016T000000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Reetika Sindhi\, University of California\, Sant
 a Barbara - The Long Shadow of Communal Violence: Childhood Exposure and
  Later-Life Mental Health
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Reetika Sindhi\, University of California\, Santa Ba
 rbara\nTitle: "The Long Shadow of Communal Violence: Childhood Exposure 
 and Later-Life Mental Health"\n\nWe study the long-run relationship betw
 een childhood exposure to communal violence and women’s mental health 
 in India. Using a newly constructed crosswalk spanning seven decades\, w
 e link historical Hindu--Muslim riot locations to respondents’ childho
 od districts in the Longitudinal Ageing Study in India (LASI). Exploitin
 g variation in exposure across childhood districts and birth cohorts\, w
 e find that exposure to at least one riot between birth and age~14 is as
 sociated with a 2.4 percentage point higher probability of major depress
 ive disorder in later life\, or 29 percent of the sample mean. Greater c
 umulative exposure is similarly associated with worse later-life mental 
 health. The association is strongest for exposure at ages 0--4 and 10--1
 4\, consistent with differences in vulnerability across developmental st
 ages. Childhood riot exposure is also associated with a higher likelihoo
 d of child marriage\, pointing to changes in life-course trajectories as
  a potential channel. These findings show that even localized\, short-li
 ved episodes of communal violence can have mental-health consequences th
 at persist for decades. JEL Classification Code: D74\, I12\, I31\, J12\,
  J14\, J16\, N35\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-t
 alk-reetika-sindhi-university-california-santa-barbara
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-reetika-sindh
 i-university-california-santa-barbara
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:4525f480e09577d97162@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20261124T003607Z
DTSTART:20261020T223000Z
DTEND:20261021T000000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Donovan Rasamoelison\, University of California\
 , Santa Barbara - “More Paperwork\, Less Fraud? The Welfare Impact of 
 Compliance Documentation Beyond Screening”
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Donovan Rasamoelison\, University of California\, Sa
 nta Barbara\nTitle: “More Paperwork\, Less Fraud? The Welfare Impact o
 f Compliance Documentation Beyond Screening”\n\nThis paper investigate
 s whether compliance documentation offers incremental fraud deterrence i
 n the presence of initial ex-ante screening by leveraging a policy featu
 re of the Paycheck Protection Program - a loan-size threshold that waive
 d compliance documentation requirements for loan forgiveness. On the one
  hand\, the waiver may encourage firms with true loan eligibility below 
 the threshold to opportunistically inflate their loan requests up to the
  threshold. On the other hand\, firms with true loan amounts above the t
 hreshold may strategically reduce their loans to avoid the administrativ
 e burden of compliance. Quantitatively\, opportunistic overclaiming was 
 limited and the loan-reducing effect outweighed the loan-increasing effe
 ct\, resulting in a net welfare gain of $0.03 per dollar borrowed from t
 he documentation waiver. These results indicate that compliance document
 ation requirements impose substantial burden while providing limited mar
 ginal fraud deterrence in the presence of initial screening.\n\nhttps://
 econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-donovan-rasamoelison-uni
 versity-california-santa-barbara
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-donovan-rasam
 oelison-university-california-santa-barbara
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:2
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:606d4b60edddc1a75ea2@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20261124T003607Z
DTSTART:20261022T223000Z
DTEND:20261023T000000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Anna Pede\, University of California\, Santa Bar
 bara - Fire and Punishment: Enforcement incentives and fire use in the B
 razilian Amazon
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Anna Pede\, University of California\, Santa Barbara
 \nTitle: "Fire and Punishment: Enforcement incentives and fire use in th
 e Brazilian Amazon"\n\nIncomplete monitoring of illegal behavior may cau
 se violators to reoptimize their criminal activities. I study how satell
 ite monitoring of deforestation impacts illegal land-clearing behavior i
 n the Brazilian Amazon. I argue that by detecting primarily mechanical c
 learings\, the introduction of near-real-time satellite monitoring favor
 ed a technological substitution towards the use of fire. Using cloud cov
 er as an instrument for satellite-based detection\, I find that municipa
 lities under more intensive surveillance experienced a statistically sig
 nificant substitution from mechanical to fire-based clearing. My finding
 s help explain why Amazon fires have persisted despite the overall decli
 ne in deforestation starting from 2005. Keywords: Forest fires\, defores
 tation\, environmental law enforcement JEL Classification Codes: Q51\, Q
 57\, Q58\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-anna
 -pede-university-california-santa-barbara
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-anna-pede-uni
 versity-california-santa-barbara
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:695624000182a6c167e9@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20261124T003607Z
DTSTART:20261113T210000Z
DTEND:20261113T223000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Ricardo Jose Ramos Fontes\, University of Califo
 rnia\, Santa Barbara - “Droughts\, Dirty Energy\, and Health”
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Ricardo Jose Ramos Fontes\, University of California
 \, Santa Barbara\nTitle: “Droughts\, Dirty Energy\, and Health”\n\nH
 ydropower-dependent electricity systems are increasingly exposed to drou
 ght risk. This paper studies one consequence of that exposure in Brazil:
  when droughts reduce the availability of hydropower\, the system operat
 ors increase electricity generation from fossil fuels\, and the resultin
 g air pollution exposure raises urgent respiratory morbidity. We combine
  data on plant-level generation\, hydrological conditions\, and ERA5 win
 d fields with hospitalization records from the public health system SUS.
  We first show that droughts reduce hydro inflows and reservoir storage 
 and that lower hydropower generation increases generation from coal\, oi
 l\, and natural gas. We construct a drought-predicted transported fossil
  generation exposure measure by combining predicted thermal dispatch wit
 h source-receptor weights based on distance and wind alignment. We then 
 estimate how increases in power generation from fossil fuels affect urge
 nt hospitalizations in nearby areas. We find that exposure to drought-in
 duced power generation from fossil fuels is associated with higher urgen
 t respiratory morbidity. Perhaps surprisingly\, we find that even power 
 generation from natural gas is associated with large and significant inc
 reases in hospitalization rates due to respiratory conditions. Finally\,
  after the expansion of wind and solar generation in the previous decade
 \, the fossil dispatch response to hydropower scarcity became weaker\; a
  counterfactual analysis suggests that this weaker response reduced drou
 ght-induced ramp-up of generation from fossil fuels and may have averted
  several hundred urgent respiratory admissions. Keywords: Drought\, Hydr
 opower\, Electricity generation\, Fossil fuels\, Respiratory health\, Br
 azil. JEL Classification Codes: I15\, O13\, Q41\, Q42\, Q53 Event Detail
 s Ricardo will be presenting his Advancement to Candidacy paper\, “Dro
 ughts\, Dirty Energy\, and Health”. To access the Advancement paper\, 
 you must have an active UCSB NetID and password.\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.ed
 u/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentation-droughts-dirty-ener
 gy-and-health-ricardo-jose
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentat
 ion-droughts-dirty-energy-and-health-ricardo-jose
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:e3991ffe7feda125dbbf@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20261124T003607Z
DTSTART:20261117T180000Z
DTEND:20261117T193000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Ryo Aono\, University of California Santa Barbar
 a - “Underprovision by Local Governments During Aging”
LOCATION:North Hall 2113
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Ryo Aono\, University of California Santa Barbara\nT
 itle: “Underprovision by Local Governments During Aging”\n\nLocal go
 vernments in developed countries increasingly struggle to maintain publi
 c goods provision as populations age. Using government finance data for 
 22 OECD countries from 1995 to 2019\, I find that\, as the dependency ra
 tio rises\, local governments tend to cut both investment in stock publi
 c goods and expenditure on flow public goods\, while central governments
  tend to cut investment slightly and increase flow public goods as well 
 as social benefits. To explain these patterns\, I build a calibrated OLG
  model in which central and local governments play a time-consistent Sta
 ckelberg game over a shared tax base\, under rigid rules for pensions an
 d grants. Rising pension obligations raise central taxes\, which\, throu
 gh a vertical tax externality and smaller grants\, constrain local reven
 ue. With more efficient central tax collection and higher substitutabili
 ty across tiers for flow than for stock public goods\, the model reprodu
 ces the observed responses: public goods provision becomes more centrali
 zed\, and composite provision declines. Moderately lowering the pension 
 replacement rate and the grant rate improves welfare by stimulating hous
 ehold savings and improving the efficiency of public resource allocation
 . JEL Classification Codes: H54\, H55\, H72\, H77\, J11\n\nhttps://econ.
 ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentation-underprovisi
 on-local-governments-during-aging
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentat
 ion-underprovision-local-governments-during-aging
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:0f2ce931a83ec5717058@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20261124T003607Z
DTSTART:20261117T180000Z
DTEND:20261117T193000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Lars Leimkühler\, University of California\, Sa
 nta Barbara - “Crossing the Line: Evidence of Spillovers from the Los 
 Angeles Mansion Tax”\,
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Lars Leimkühler\, University of California\, Santa 
 Barbara\nTitle: “Crossing the Line: Evidence of Spillovers from the Lo
 s Angeles Mansion Tax”\,\n\nReal estate transfer taxes on high-end hou
 sing are increasingly popular revenue generation tools for local governm
 ents. Yet\, their fiscal promise depends on whether wealthy buyers’ lo
 cational preferences are sufficient to keep them inside the taxed jurisd
 iction\, or whether the tax induces relocation to neighboring untaxed ju
 risdictions. This paper provides the first direct evidence on the spatia
 l scope and magnitude of cross-jurisdictional spillovers by exploiting t
 he implementation of a 4% local transfer tax. Using transaction-level da
 ta in a difference-in-differences and spatial RD setting\, I find that d
 espite the abundant availability of tax-exempt substitutes nearby\, spil
 lovers recover just 6 percentage points of the 34% decline in sales in t
 axed areas. These spillovers are concentrated within 500 meters of the t
 axed city’s boundary\, decay with increasing distance\, and are most p
 ronounced near tax-exempt enclaves such as Beverly Hills and Santa Monic
 a. The resulting fiscal externality is modest. JEL Classification Codes:
  H71\, H73\, R21\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-ca
 ndidacy-presentation-crossing-line-evidence-spillovers-los-angeles
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentat
 ion-crossing-line-evidence-spillovers-los-angeles
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:9e83e2af79e2292d55f1@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20261124T003607Z
DTSTART:20261124T180000Z
DTEND:20261124T193000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Xiyue Liu\, University of California\, Santa Bar
 bara - “Urban and Rural Disparity in China Marriage Market”
LOCATION:North Hall 2113
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Xiyue Liu\, University of California\, Santa Barbara
 \nTitle: “Urban and Rural Disparity in China Marriage Market”\n\nMar
 riage Market in China has become a heated topic\, and a skewed sex ratio
  is a known cause of troubles. Compounded with assortative matching and 
 urban-rural disparity in marriage market\, the result was surprisingly u
 neven\, even with women nominally favored by the sex ratio. I investigat
 ed the difficulties singles face\, and focused on the contrast between u
 rban and rural area in China\, in particular the claim that well-educate
 d women struggle to marry in city\, while less educated men languish in 
 rural area even more\, and find supporting evidences from census data. A
 fter this\, using 2010\, 2020\, and the most recent 2022 CFPS data I inv
 estigated matching patterns\, in urban area\, rural area\, and combined.
  To interpreted changes beside sex ratio\, I evaluated marriage surplus 
 i.e.\, the value of marriage\, by Choo and Siow (2006) model to understa
 nd aggregated demand of singles by education level\, after disentangling
  effect of sex ratio. Adding to Rossi and Xiao (2025)’s work\, I compa
 red urban and rural marriage market in China to exploit variations over 
 percent of people with high school education or above\, identified a sur
 prising difference in sorting pattern\, that in rural couple who both ha
 ve high school or above level of education have lower surplus than when 
 only man does\, contrary to the city’s consistent positive sorting pat
 tern.\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-pre
 sentation-urban-and-rural-disparity-china-marriage-market
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentat
 ion-urban-and-rural-disparity-china-marriage-market
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:501d73a40a0a870e8bdf@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20261124T003607Z
DTSTART:20261130T180000Z
DTEND:20261130T193000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Yu Xu\, University of California\, Santa Barbara
  - “From Business Tax to VAT: Firm Boundaries in Business Groups”
LOCATION:North Hall 2212
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Yu Xu\, University of California\, Santa Barbara\nTi
 tle: “From Business Tax to VAT: Firm Boundaries in Business Groups”\
 n\nChina’s business tax fell on every sale between two legal entities\
 , including sales between a firm and its own subsidiaries. We study how 
 manufacturing groups responded when the business tax on transport and si
 x modern services was replaced by a value-added tax in 2012–2013. Usin
 g the subsidiary lists of 793 listed manufacturing firms matched to the 
 business registry\, we find that groups moved more of these services int
 o subsidiaries of their own: the number of new subsidiaries in the refor
 med services per year nearly tripled\, while new manufacturing and whole
 sale subsidiaries did not increase. Groups more exposed to the reform th
 rough input-output links set up more subsidiaries in businesses related 
 to the parent’s. Groups that separated more services also raised their
  revenue from services sold outside the group and their share of technic
 al staff more than other groups. The business scope and the patents\, so
 ftware copyrights and trademarks of the new subsidiaries show what lies 
 behind these changes: the new entities took on related service work and 
 entered new service and technology businesses\, while the parent kept th
 e core assets. Keywords: business tax\, business groups\, legal entities
 \, firm boundaries JEL Classification Codes: H25\, H32\, L22\, L23\, L24
 \, O14\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-pr
 esentation-business-tax-vat-firm-boundaries-business-groups
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentat
 ion-business-tax-vat-firm-boundaries-business-groups
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:08abb562e6bda836da4e@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20261124T003607Z
DTSTART:20261130T233000Z
DTEND:20261201T010000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Xinlu Zhao\, University of California\, Santa Ba
 rbara - “Candidate AI Assistance in Job Interviews: Recruiter Judgment
  and Matching Quality”
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Xinlu Zhao\, University of California\, Santa Barbar
 a\nTitle: “Candidate AI Assistance in Job Interviews: Recruiter Judgme
 nt and Matching Quality”\n\nGenerative AI tools can now suggest answer
 s to job candidates in real time during interviews. Such assistance may 
 help candidates show what they know\, but it may also make interview per
 formance less informative about their underlying ability. This paper stu
 dies how real-time AI assistance shapes recruiters&#39\; judgments\, com
 bining a pilot experiment with the design of a larger second round. In t
 he pilot\, 24 undergraduate candidates completed an unaided written job 
 task and then interviewed with one of four professional recruiters\, who
  were not told that the study concerned AI. Within grade point average (
 GPA) strata\, candidates were randomly assigned to interview with or wit
 hout a real-time AI assistant\, and recruiters predicted each candidate'
 s task score before and after the interview. Candidates with AI access r
 eceived interview ratings 1.25 control-group standard deviations higher.
  Recruiters revised their predictions upward for AI-access candidates an
 d downward for control candidates\, while the interview improved predict
 ion accuracy by similar amounts in both groups. The second round extends
  the design to selection: each recruiter interviews ten candidates\, hal
 f of whom receive AI access within pairs matched on a pre-assignment tas
 k score\, and ranks them for three positions. It estimates whether AI ac
 cess raises candidates' ranks at a given task score and whether it chang
 es how closely rankings track unaided task performance\, the two channel
 s through which AI assistance can affect matching quality. JEL classific
 ation codes: C93\, D82\, D83\, J24\, M51 Keywords: candidate AI assistan
 ce\; interviews\; recruiter learning\; work samples\; matching quality\n
 \nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentati
 on-candidate-ai-assistance-job-interviews-recruiter
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentat
 ion-candidate-ai-assistance-job-interviews-recruiter
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:3decd17dbf16ab3ff75c@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20261124T003607Z
DTSTART:20261201T180000Z
DTEND:20261201T193000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Kyle Araki-Chew\, University of California\, San
 ta Barbara - “Wealth Inequality as Structural Access”
LOCATION:North Hall 2113
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Kyle Araki-Chew\, University of California\, Santa B
 arbara\nTitle: “Wealth Inequality as Structural Access”\n\nReturns o
 n wealth rise with wealth\, and models of the wealth distribution usuall
 y attribute this gap to traits of households. This paper asks how much o
 f the gap\, and of wealth concentration\, comes instead from the rules t
 hat decide which savings tools each household can use: the prices of cre
 dit\, cash and stock ownership\, the rules of 401(k)s and Roth IRAs\, an
 d the accredited-investor test. I build a model of ex ante identical hou
 seholds whose saving follows imposed rules and whose portfolios are chos
 en optimally. The model economy lives through the actual 1928–2025 U.S
 . market history\, with each rule on its real calendar\, and is fitted t
 o the Survey of Consumer Finances 2016–2025. The prices of access to c
 redit\, cash and stocks produce the losses at the indebted bottom of the
  U.S. return gap and 38–49% of its climb above the median. Private equ
 ity in IRAs makes the Roth IRA’s tax benefit top-heavy. A top-1% house
 hold’s Roth escapes about 25 times the tax of a 50th–90th household
 ’s\; barring private equity from IRAs would cut the top 1%’s share o
 f the benefit from 22% to 9% and leave the middle’s untouched. Yet Rot
 hs hold the 2025 top 1% share of financial wealth 0.5–0.8 percentage p
 oints lower and add $0.9–1.6 trillion of household wealth\, more than 
 half of it in the 50th–90th percentiles\, because the contribution cap
  keeps Roth wealth small at the top. The private-equity access rules mov
 e the top 1% share by 0.2 percentage points or less\, because one fund
 ’s risk keeps household stakes small. Putting 10% private equity in 40
 1(k) default funds from 2006 would have lowered the top 1% share by 0.3 
 percentage points and raised tax revenue. JEL Classification Codes: D31\
 , E21\, G11\, G28\, G51\, H24\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/a
 dvancement-candidacy-presentation-wealth-inequality-structural-access-ky
 le-araki
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentat
 ion-wealth-inequality-structural-access-kyle-araki
