SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Hung Kit (William) Chiu\, University of Californ
 ia\, Santa Barbara - “The Rise and Fall of the Car Break-in Epidemic i
 n San Francisco and Oakland”
LOCATION:North Hall 2212
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Hung Kit (William) Chiu\, University of California\,
  Santa Barbara\nTitle: “The Rise and Fall of the Car Break-in Epidemic
  in San Francisco and Oakland”\n\nCar break-ins in San Francisco and O
 akland roughly tripled between 2010 and 2017\, a surge widely blamed on 
 California's criminal justice reforms. Against synthetic controls built 
 from 49 other California cities\, break-ins were 55% higher in San Franc
 isco and 91% higher in Oakland in 2011–14\, with little excess in burg
 lary or car theft. That rules out a law acting alike across the state\, 
 and no other American city of 100\,000 or more saw a rise in break-ins p
 er resident of that size. The surge began in Oakland\, which laid off a 
 tenth of its officers in July 2010 and announced that it would no longer
  answer most property crimes\, and the epidemic ended after enforcement 
 stepped up in both cities in 2023–24: San Francisco's break-ins fell b
 y more than four fifths between 2023 and 2026\, most near its visitor la
 ndmarks and far faster than robbery or burglary\, and the fall began mor
 e than a year before Proposition 36 restored tougher penalties. On the d
 ay\, break-ins follow parked cars and avoid police\, and the city's tota
 l moves with them. A model in which crews\, which I take as a working hy
 pothesis\, decide daily whether to go out and monthly whether to form or
  quit accounts for both episodes. In it\, the long-run effect of San Fra
 ncisco's crackdown is more than two and a half times its effect in the f
 irst months. JEL Classification Codes: K42 (Illegal Behavior and the Enf
 orcement of Law)\; R10 (General Regional Economics)\; C25 (Discrete Regr
 ession and Qualitative Choice Models). Keywords: Crime\; Car break-ins\;
  Criminal justice reform\; Place-based policing\; Nested logit.\n\nhttps
 ://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentation-rise
 -and-fall-car-break-epidemic-san-francisco
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentat
 ion-rise-and-fall-car-break-epidemic-san-francisco
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:0
