SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Qijun (Liona) Liu\, University of California\, S
 anta Barbara - “Losing a Star PI: Trainees' Research Output and Career
 s”
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Qijun (Liona) Liu\, University of California\, Santa
  Barbara\nTitle: “Losing a Star PI: Trainees' Research Output and Care
 ers”\n\nWe study the deaths of eminent principal investigators (PIs) i
 n U.S. life sciences between 1986 and 2009. We identify junior researche
 rs from publications on which they are first authors\, and the deceased 
 PI is last author\, and compare their outcomes with those of trainees li
 nked to comparable alive PIs using coarsened exact matching and differen
 ce-in-differences. Among trainees whose first qualifying first–last-au
 thored paper appeared one of two years before the death\, annual journal
  publications decline by an estimated 0.17 articles (15 percent) over th
 e following five years\, while impact-weighted output falls by 19 percen
 t. The decline is primarily concentrated in publications with the deceas
 ed PI\, with no statistically detectable increase in publications withou
 t the PI. We also estimate a 7.4-percentage-point lower probability of r
 eaching first last-authorship six to fifteen years after th death. These
  findings highlight the importance of senior–junior research relations
 hips for scientific production and early-career development. Keywords: S
 cientific collaboration\, Principal investigator death\, Research produc
 tivity\, Early-career researchers JEL Classification Codes: O31\, J24\, 
 I23\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-prese
 ntation-losing-star-pi-trainees-research-output-and
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentat
 ion-losing-star-pi-trainees-research-output-and
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:1
