UID:ee69fbb327551c7b6427@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20261124T003607Z
DTSTART:20261208T180000Z
DTEND:20261208T193000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Shan Lu\, University of California\, Santa Barba
 ra - “Updating from Categorical Signals: An Experiment”
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Shan Lu\, University of California\, Santa Barbara\n
 Title: “Updating from Categorical Signals: An Experiment”\n\nMany co
 nsequential signals say only whether an unobserved quantity lies above o
 r below a threshold: a home is inside or outside a flood zone\, a test i
 s positive or negative\, earnings beat or miss a forecast. The Bayesian 
 response to such a signal is to truncate one’s belief at the threshold
 . I study how well people carry out this truncation\, in a laboratory ex
 periment in which the prior is displayed\, the signal is noiseless and t
 he Bayesian answer is known. Subjects estimate a number drawn from a dis
 played distribution before and after learning which side of a threshold 
 it falls on\, once when they must work out what the signal implies and o
 nce when the implied distribution is shown to them. The design separates
  three ways in which the inference can fail: a general departure from th
 e Bayesian update after the category (in either direction)\, neglect of 
 a signal that confirms what the prior already suggested\, and attention 
 to the category at the expense of the distribution inside it. The result
 s bear on how risk should be disclosed\, and on models in economics that
  take the Bayesian use of coarse information as a primitive. JEL Classif
 ication Codes: C91\, D81\, D83\, D91\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all
 /2026/advancement-candidacy-presentation-updating-categorical-signals-ex
 periment-shan-lu
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/advancement-candidacy-presentat
 ion-updating-categorical-signals-experiment-shan-lu
